{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And everyone who remains</b> — This is the third one he mentioned. Because he said לְמֶלֶךְ (\"to a king\") with a shva under the lamed and the lamed is not pointed [with a kamatz gadol], it appears that it is in construct state. The Name is the Messiah, in the manner of \"and I have set My king\" (Psalms 2:6), and it is written: \"all nations shall bow down to him\" (Genesis 49:10) — either concerning Solomon or concerning the Messiah, His son."], :book :zechariah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The argument turns on the vocalization of לְמֶלֶךְ in the preceding verse (Zechariah 14:15): a shva under the lamed indicates construct state rather than the independent form לַמֶּלֶךְ (with dagesh and kamatz gadol). The phrase על שלמה או על המשיח בנו echoes Psalms 2:7, where בני refers to the king/Messiah."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Zecharia -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/zecharya"] ["1" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=25&perek=5&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:47:08.137686608Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}